  "narration": [
    {
      "id": "brief",
      "text": "The Wrong Kite, in São Paulo. The hat shop works: three visitors browse, log in and buy. Three red Cloudlings have come too: one from an address range on a block list, one hiding a script in a review, one flooding the shop. Click the clouds and read their cards, then fly the kites.",
      "highlight": "button:fly",
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "run-finished"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "what-happened",
      "text": "All three got in. A security group can only allow, never deny, and neither it nor a network ACL can see inside a request. Two defences will fix that: a [network ACL](card:network-acl) at the subnet edge to turn away the block-listed range, and [AWS WAF](card:waf-web-acl) to read each request and count how many arrive.",
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "acknowledged"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "associate",
      "text": "First, the edge. The VPC has a second network ACL, edge-acl, made for the public subnets. Click Public subnet A and switch it to edge-acl, then Public subnet B. Then fly the kites and watch what a custom ACL does with traffic it has no rule for.",
      "highlight": ["subnet:public-a", "subnet:public-b"],
      "pulse": ["subnet-network-acl:public-a", "subnet-network-acl:public-b"],
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "run-finished",
        "predicate": {
          "kind": "network-acl-on-subnets",
          "networkAclId": "edge-acl",
          "subnetIds": ["public-a", "public-b"]
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "allow-https",
      "text": "Every kite tangled in the ribbon: edge-acl has no rule for HTTPS from the internet, so its final * rule denied them all. A custom ACL denies whatever it is not told to allow. Add an inbound rule: number 100, allow HTTPS 443 from anywhere. Then fly again.",
      "highlight": "subnet:public-a",
      "pulse": ["network-acl:edge-acl"],
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "run-finished",
        "predicate": {
          "kind": "network-acl-allows",
          "networkAclId": "edge-acl",
          "direction": "inbound",
          "ports": "https",
          "peer": {
            "kind": "anywhere"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "stateless",
      "text": "The requests got in, but every reply was dropped on its way out: follow a green kite to see where. A network ACL is stateless, so a reply needs its own rule. Add an outbound rule: number 100, allow Ephemeral 1024–65535 to anywhere (the two rules numbered 200 are the same pair, for the web servers). Then fly again.",
      "highlight": "subnet:public-a",
      "pulse": ["network-acl:edge-acl"],
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "run-finished",
        "predicate": {
          "kind": "flows-delivered",
          "flows": {
            "intent": "legitimate"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "deny-range",
      "text": "Visitors get in and out, and so does the block-listed range. Add an inbound rule denying All traffic from 198.51.100.0/24, numbered below 100: rules are read lowest first and the first match decides, so a deny after the allow is never read. Then fly.",
      "highlight": "subnet:public-a",
      "pulse": ["network-acl:edge-acl"],
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "run-finished",
        "predicate": {
          "kind": "flows-stopped",
          "flows": {
            "flowIds": ["red-range"]
          },
          "by": "nacl"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "waf",
      "text": "The other two arrive like any visitor: HTTPS 443, ordinary addresses. Only AWS WAF reads the requests themselves. Drag a WAF web ACL from the tray onto the load balancer, then switch on the Core rule set and a rate-based rule.",
      "highlight": "palette:shop-waf",
      "pulse": ["web-acl-rules:shop-waf"],
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "scene",
        "predicate": {
          "kind": "web-acl-protects",
          "loadBalancerId": "alb",
          "rules": ["core-rule-set", "rate-based"]
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "second-run",
      "text": "Fly the kites and watch the net beneath the load balancer: the Core rule set catches the hidden script, and the rate-based rule catches the flood. Click any kite to follow it through the edge, the security group, the listener and WAF.",
      "highlight": "button:fly",
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "run-finished"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "wrap",
      "text": "Each attack stopped at the [layer that can see it](card:concept-defence-in-depth): the range at the subnet edge, the script and the flood in WAF. No third star yet? Leave room between rule numbers, at least 10 apart like 90, 100, 110, so a rule can go between any two later without renumbering.",
      "pulse": ["network-acl:edge-acl"],
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "level-complete"
      }
    }
  ],
